				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/backelaer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1323848); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1323848&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608435315" alt="Backelaer" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1323848)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/backelaer" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1323848); return false;" >Backelaer</a></span> <span class="odate time_1340673540 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Jun 2012 01:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1490195">
									

<p>*sigh* Yes your point is pretty obvious.</p>
<p>(warning: rant mode on) There are some truly great stories and scp's on this site. Products of great creativity, that can really make your mind go wild. But I always felt there is also a downside. Something that really pushes my buttons. And that is <em>people trying to be smart.</em> Be it in glorifying a certain scp, or burning it down to the ground, there just seems to be a lot of folks trying so, <em>so</em> hard to come across as smart and witty in the discussion pages.</p>
<p>Well if you're so smart and you don't like a certain article, just say so. Or write something better for fuck's sake. And if you like it, just fucking say so too. Don't sit and ponder about a smart way to praise it. The discussion page should be used for constructive criticism, and/or constructive praise. Not to show the rest of the world how witty and smart you are. /rant mode off</p>
<p>Jekeled, this is in no way directed at you, but you bringing up Hemingway kinda sparked it. Because yes, a four word story can be brilliant, but for god's sake, as brilliant as it is, I've heard that Hemingway story way too much. And I recon the rest of the world knows it to. Bringing that up doesn't make you particularly intelligent. And this particular story doesn't even come close to Hemingways for that matter.</p>
<p>Regarding this article; too much purple prose, and not really interesting.</p>

							</div>

								
					
